# Ignore rules for the Fit project root.
#
# Two of these are not housekeeping — they are the reason this file exists at all.

# THE THROWAWAY PLATFORM SPIKE. Its own git repository on a public branch, kept only for the
# findings it produced. Its code is discarded and must never become part of the application.
/spike/

# PRESERVED SPIKE EVIDENCE. Deliberately untracked and MUST NEVER BE COMMITTED ANYWHERE: it
# quotes the coach's real Drive identifiers, calendar identifiers and signed-in account. The
# repository is public by a knowing decision, so anything here reaching it is a real leak, and one
# that no credential scanner would catch because none of it is shaped like a credential.
/_spike-evidence/

# Dependencies. Restored with `npm install`, never committed.
node_modules/

# Local environment and editor noise.
.env
.env.*
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.log

# IGNORED, AND THIS RULE REPLACES THE OPPOSITE ONE. Until 1 August 2026 this file carried a
# NOT-IGNORED note for app/dist/, and that note was CORRECT for the host it was written about: a
# static host that serves a branch's contents has no build step of its own, so the built output
# had to be in the repository or the address would serve an empty site.
#
# THE HOST CHANGED. The published site is now built by GitHub Actions — `.github/workflows/pages.yml`
# runs `npm ci` and `npm run build` on ubuntu and uploads what IT built as the Pages artifact. CI
# BUILDS FROM SOURCE AND NEVER READS THE COMMITTED app/dist. Measured: the live page at
# https://visak13.github.io/Fit/ executes `index-BGG4vweb.js`, while the artefact this repository
# used to track was `index-BDreLcB9.js` — a different file, from the same source, built on a
# different machine.
#
# So tracking it bought nothing and cost something real: a hand-applied fix to the committed
# bundle would pass every local check and every review and still never reach the coach, because
# nothing on the publish path ever opens it. `npm run build` still writes app/dist/ locally and
# `npm run check:stale` still reports whether that local copy matches local source — both are
# about your own machine now, not about what is served.
app/dist/
